WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.888
ShellCheck analyzes scripts without
running them.

2
00:00:04.968 --> 00:00:09.455
That makes it useful early in development
and review,

3
00:00:09.535 --> 00:00:15.619
because it can point out risky patterns
before the script touches files,

4
00:00:15.699 --> 00:00:18.591
services, or production systems.

5
00:00:18.671 --> 00:00:23.856
It examines shell syntax, variable use,
quoting, expansion,

6
00:00:23.936 --> 00:00:29.521
command structure, conditionals, and
portability assumptions.

7
00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:36.382
It also provides maintainability guidance
where a construct may be confusing to

8
00:00:36.462 --> 00:00:37.858
future readers.

9
00:00:37.938 --> 00:00:42.924
Many findings are about shell behavior
that is easy to miss.

10
00:00:43.004 --> 00:00:49.786
ShellCheck can warn when an unquoted value
may be split into multiple arguments,

11
00:00:49.866 --> 00:00:53.555
when a variable looks misspelled or
unused,

12
00:00:53.635 --> 00:00:56.826
when a test expression is suspicious,

13
00:00:56.906 --> 00:01:00.696
when command substitution may hide an
error,

14
00:01:00.776 --> 00:01:05.064
or when a feature does not match the
declared shell.

15
00:01:05.144 --> 00:01:08.535
These are not obscure academic details.

16
00:01:08.615 --> 00:01:15.496
They are the kinds of issues that make
automation behave differently from what the

17
00:01:15.576 --> 00:01:17.072
author intended.

18
00:01:17.152 --> 00:01:19.545
Static analysis has limits.

19
00:01:19.625 --> 00:01:25.509
ShellCheck does not execute the workflow,
contact the remote system,

20
00:01:25.589 --> 00:01:32.470
inspect the production policy, or know the
sensitivity of the data being handled.

21
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:39.231
It may not understand why a team
intentionally chose one behavior over another.

22
00:01:39.311 --> 00:01:44.995
It cannot prove that a deployment is safe,
that a backup is complete,

23
00:01:45.075 --> 00:01:51.059
or that an incident-response script meets
operational requirements.

24
00:01:51.139 --> 00:01:57.721
Runtime testing, human review, and
operational validation are still needed.

25
00:01:57.801 --> 00:02:03.485
The best mindset is to treat ShellCheck
findings as review prompts.

26
00:02:03.565 --> 00:02:09.050
Some findings are straightforward and
should be fixed directly.

27
00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:16.210
Others reveal assumptions that need
documentation, a test case, or a design change.

28
00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:20.977
A finding can also help reviewers ask
better questions:

29
00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:27.140
what shell will run this script, what
happens when the variable is empty,

30
00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.312
what happens when the command fails,

31
00:02:30.392 --> 00:02:34.580
and does this code preserve argument
boundaries?
